@meta
id: 11-01
title: The key that does it again
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-repeat
par: 27
minutes: 4
vscode: select the column of plus signs with Alt+click, or delete ten times
open: imports.ts

@brief
Someone pasted this import block straight out of a diff, so every line
still carries the `+` that the diff put there. Ten lines, ten stray
characters, one at the front of each line.

Take all ten `+` characters out. Nothing else on the lines changes.

You already know how to delete a character. The point of this lesson is
what you press for the second one, and the third, and the tenth.

@keys
| `x` | delete the character under the cursor |
| `.` | repeat the last change |
| `j` | down one line, keeping the column |

@hints
Delete the first `+` the way you already know. Then look for a way to say
"that again" instead of typing the same command out a second time.
--
`.` — the full stop — repeats the last change you made. Not the last
motion, the last change. Moving the cursor does not disturb it.
--
`x` once on line one. Then `j.` gets you the next line, and `j.` again
after that. The `.` never needs to be re-aimed; you only ever move.

@solution
    x  then  j.  nine times

The cursor starts on the `+` on line one. `x` removes it and vim quietly
remembers that change. `j` moves down a line without changing anything,
so the memory survives, and `.` replays the delete at the new cursor
position.

Two keys per line instead of one key plus a fresh decision per line. That
sounds like a small saving and it is not, because the real cost of an
edit is not the keystroke — it is the thinking. `j.` is one thought
repeated. Retyping `x` ten times is ten chances to lose your place.

`.` is the most valuable key in vim and it is almost free to learn. What
takes skill, and what the rest of this chapter is about, is arranging
your edits so that `.` is the right answer more often.

